From e7a28c1fc68c13eebc4b302f569323f3e7dc3d9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 18:07:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 60/61] inline-3d(Phase 1): match an inline-3D canvas to its
 quad by identity
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Builds the canvas half of the identity join on top of the plumbing patch,
and fixes the reason it could never have worked before.

THE JOIN DID NOT EXIST NATURALLY. A kInline3dWeave tracked rect rides the
element's BACKGROUND paint chunk — ReplacedPainter forces one onto an
otherwise undecorated canvas for exactly that purpose — and that chunk
merges into the enclosing PictureLayer. The canvas PIXELS, meanwhile, are
a ForeignLayer from HTMLCanvasElement::ContentsCcLayer(), which
PendingLayer::RequiresOwnLayer() always keeps separate. Two LayerImpls,
two stable ids. LayerTreeHostImpl already says as much out loud, in the
comment about the inline-3d chunk landing on "a layer with no drawable
content".

So HTMLCanvasPainter now registers the rect directly on the ForeignLayer
(layer-local, cc projects it through the layer's ScreenSpaceTransform at
draw time), and ReplacedPainter stops forcing the background item for a
canvas whose ONLY tracked feature is kInline3dWeave — the branch stays
for region capture and the other features. Emitting both copies would
hand the same token two different layer ids every frame.

The aggregator then:
  - carries {rect, element token, sqs_layer_id, namespace} per weave rect
    instead of a bare rect, on both channels (the legacy metadata channel
    supplies zeroes, which is the documented "no identity" case);
  - sorts ID-FIRST. The old (y,x,w,h) order was stable only while nothing
    moved: two tiles crossing during a scroll swapped indices, and the
    index-keyed GPU state followed the wrong tile for a frame. Geometry
    stays as the tiebreak so legacy rects keep today's order;
  - tries an exact (namespace, layer id) match before the geometric
    best_match, which is kept verbatim as the fallback. When the exact
    join cannot be used it logs a throttled "inline-3D ID-MATCH FALLBACK"
    with a reason string, because the two reasons — nothing stamped vs
    stamped-but-unclaimed — need different follow-ups;
  - collapses duplicate ids. Reachable on an ordinary page: a canvas that
    also has a background colour, a touch-action region, or a wheel
    handler still records its background chunk, so both copies ship. The
    collapse keeps the one whose layer id actually names a root quad. It
    is deliberately not a DCHECK — this must degrade, not crash.

The token then rides the whole way to the GPU: AggregatedFrame's weave
rects and canvas resources, SkiaOutputSurface::Inline3dRect and
Inline3dLayerMailbox. Nothing keys off it yet — the GPU stage still
tracks targets positionally and projects the identity away — that is the
next patch. DirectRenderer (the mac overlay-substitution path) only ever
wanted geometry, so Display projects at the call site rather than churn
its interface.

Token persistence across close(): close() must still clear the element's
registration, or a dormant tile would keep emitting weave rects. But the
SDK re-creates an XRDisplayLayer for the same canvas on every lazy
re-activation, and a fresh random token each time would orphan the
GPU-side per-target state on every scroll. Rather than cache the token
(Element is not Supplementable, and a static weak map or a new core
rare-data field are both heavier than the problem), XRDisplayLayer now
DERIVES it: a per-renderer-process random high word, minted once, plus
the element's DOMNodeId as the low word. Stable for the element's
lifetime, unique across the several renderers one Viz Display aggregates,
and structurally correct — there is no cache to miss or outlive the
element.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
